{"packet_id":"670ef5c03ec1b2ef","dimensions":{"problem_framing":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A1"],"reason":"A1 separates the objective (whether evidence justifies a consumer proposition) from the suggested solution. It lists real alternatives, including retail partnership and licensing. It does not identify the constraint a good analyst would not want missed: the Grenfell finding against Kingspan's own safety marketing (kingspan-grenfell-k15). The €20,000 / 12-week budget is an assumed constraint, not sourced."},"causal_mechanism":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"T1 (A9) states who changes behaviour (homeowners), why (safety concern), and how Kingspan captures value (premium pricing). The chain is not complete. It never addresses whether homeowners would trust Kingspan specifically on safety, given the dossier's 'knowingly created a false market' finding. It also never addresses whether homeowners can buy and install the product themselves; kingspan-est-rafters says rigid board rafter insulation 'isn't a DIY job'."},"distinct_theories":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"T1 (safety motive) and T2 (energy-cost motive) are genuine rival explanations of what would drive purchase. Both imply the same customer, the same product type and the same channel, differing only in messaging. There is no rival covering low or absent consumer demand, which kingspan-cotality-intent suggests."},"assumptions_predictions":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"Assumptions and predictions are observable, such as willingness-to-pay premiums and purchase intent by label. The 15% and 10% thresholds are proposed assumptions, not observations. Some predictions are vague and not clearly falsifying, for example that sales will increase in regions with strict codes."},"evidence_judgement":{"status":"scored","score":0,"event_ids":["A11","A12","A14","A15"],"reason":"Sources are misread. A11 cites the Grenfell 'knowingly created a false market' finding as SUPPORTING a fire-safety premium theory, which reverses its implication for consumer trust. It also mislabels this verified news observation as an 'independent_study' and says it 'only indicates Kingspan's marketing strategy'. A12 and A14 cite company earnings and residential outlook excerpts as support for homeowner energy-cost motivation, which they do not address; A14 also copies an inapplicable limit statement. A15 counts a company marketing claim as support for demand. Source classes are misreported throughout."},"seeking_challenge":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A13","A4"],"reason":"A13 runs one search against T1 but never reports what it found. The run then ignores contrary dossier evidence it had already retrieved in A4: kingspan-cotality-intent (only 6.3% of homeowners plan to insulate) and kingspan-est-rafters (rigid board rafter work 'isn't a DIY job'). Kingspan-bq-marketplace (online only, not stocked in stores), which bears on consumer reach, is also never assessed. It looked, but did not engage with or fairly report the challenges."},"experiment_design":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A16"],"reason":"A16 compares the fire-resistant and energy-efficient variants, but the design cannot settle the decision. The measure scale ('1-1% to 7-1%') is incoherent. A relative comparison cannot show whether any consumer demand exists. The decision rule acts only on a T1 win and treats a T2 win as 'inconclusive'. It also never tests trust in the Kingspan brand on safety. Cost and timing are proposals, not results."},"revision":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A18"],"reason":"A18 correctly judges simulated SYN-D (label printer packing errors) irrelevant to T1 and justifiably retains the theory. It does not trace any implication for the recommendation."},"recommendation":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A17","A19"],"reason":"A19 recommends investing in a fire-safety consumer proposition and rates T1 'likely', but no dossier evidence supports that rating. The recommendation conflicts with the Grenfell finding and is internally inconsistent: it says 'invest' while also making investment conditional on the survey. It does name a next action and a reversal condition, but the position itself is unsupported."}},"red_flags":[{"kind":"misleading_quote","event_ids":["A11"],"reason":"The Grenfell report finding that Kingspan 'knowingly created a false market' for K15 is presented as support for a theory that homeowners will pay a premium for Kingspan's fire-safety branding. This reverses the source's implication."},{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A11","A12","A14","A17","A19"],"reason":"Earnings and residential outlook excerpts (A12, A14) and the Grenfell finding (A11) are cited as support for homeowner purchase motives they do not address. T1 is then rated 'likely' with no supporting evidence (A17, A19)."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A15"],"reason":"A company marketing claim (kingspan-homeowner-diy) is treated as supporting evidence for demand. It is also contradicted by kingspan-est-rafters, which was never assessed."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A13","A4","A19"],"reason":"Contrary dossier evidence on low homeowner insulation intent, non-DIY installation and online-only retail reach was retrieved but ignored. The challenge step reports no findings."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A19"],"reason":"Hypothesis stated as fact: 'existing safety-certified insulation' marketed as compliance-critical is asserted despite the dossier's false-market finding."},{"kind":"non_discriminating_test","event_ids":["A16"],"reason":"The test cannot settle the investment question. The measure scale is incoherent, the design compares only two messages without a no-demand baseline, and the decision rule treats a T2 win as inconclusive, so it cannot discriminate as specified."}],"suitable_for_training":false,"screen_reason":"The episode is complete but contains a critical misreading of the Grenfell source used as support (A11), several unsupported inferences from irrelevant sources (A12, A14), a marketing claim used as evidence of demand (A15), ignored contrary evidence, and a flawed test design (A16). The final recommendation (A19) is not supported by the dossier."}
